#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Pull requests for tickets: open a draft PR from a ticket worktree, post the reviewer's findings inline, wait on
the checks, mark it ready, read the comments, list the PRs awaiting the user, run ultrareview, and land it
(squash merge, then the worktree, the branch, the main checkout and the ticket).

Usage:
    harness-pr open N [--worktree PATH] [--verify-log F] [--review F] [--notes F] [--json]
    harness-pr review P --findings F [--checked TEXT] [--model NAME]
    harness-pr findings N [--worktree PATH] --review F [--rulings R] [--model NAME] [--json]
    harness-pr findings N --latest [--json]
    harness-pr checks P [--wait] [--timeout 1200] [--json]
    harness-pr ready P [--ticket N]
    harness-pr comments P [--since ISO] [--unresolved] [--json]
    harness-pr awaiting [--all-projects] [--json]
    harness-pr ultra P [--post] [--force]
    harness-pr land P [--yes] [--discard] [--accept-findings]
    harness-pr gate P [--json]
    harness-pr revert P|SHA [--reason TEXT] [--evidence-file F] [--json]

revert undoes a landed PR on main through the main checkout (on main, no tracked changes, no commit origin/main lacks
but this revert; fetched and fast-forwarded first): `git revert --no-edit` of its merge commit and `git push origin main` (a rejected push gets one
`git pull --rebase`), an incident bug under the ticket's epic, the ticket reopened as needs-input, a notification.
A step an earlier run did is skipped. autoland calls revert_landed() in-process; the work loop never runs it.

ready and land refuse a PR whose latest harness review (the newest review carrying the harness marker) has high
or medium findings and no commit newer than it, naming those findings, and refuse when gh cannot tell. A finding
without a recognisable severity counts as high; low findings never refuse, and ready names them in one warning
line. When the review's summary counts more high plus medium findings than it lists, the refusal says how many
more. --accept-findings on land is the only override; ready has none. land on a PR that is already merged skips
the check.

findings splits a review by the files the ticket branch changed (`git diff --name-only` from the merge-base of HEAD
and origin/main, else main): high and medium on a changed file block, low on one is low, anything else is outside.
It records the round (harness_lib/reviews.py; the same HEAD replaces the latest round), writes
round-<k>-blocking.json, round-<k>-review.json and notes.md (Low findings, Out of scope, Rulings, gathered over
every round) under ~/.claude/harness/repos/<key>/reviews/N/, and prints the `harness-work stage` record: review
pass in round 1, then fix pass or fail. A review with no findings array exits 1 and records nothing; --latest
prints the latest round and writes nothing (and makes no gh call). With an open PR for the branch (`gh pr list
--head <branch> --state open`) it posts round-<k>-review.json there as `review` would (post_review) and records the
PR and the review id; a PR head that is not the worktree's HEAD (or gh failing) refuses before anything is posted or
recorded; no open PR records the round with a warning and posts nothing. Then, with an open PR, each high or medium
outside finding of any recorded round not ticketed yet (by path and body, kept in tickets.json) becomes a proposed
bug ticket under the ticket's epic (`harness-board task create`, the body on stdin, naming this round's PR), its
number on the notes line; a failed create is a warning, and the next round with a PR tries it again.

ultra runs nothing (one line, exit 0) when the project's HARNESS_ULTRAREVIEW is off, unless --force; see cmd_ultra.

The repository is the cwd's (open: --worktree, else the cwd), else CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR. gh is HARNESS_GH when
set, else `gh`; claude is HARNESS_CLAUDE, else `claude`; harness-board, harness-red and harness-work (land's Cost
block on each Resolution; a failed cost run is a warning) are found on PATH, else
next to this script. A harness-board call that succeeds passes its `harness-board: warning:` stderr lines through
to stderr, in order; its other stderr stays hidden. HARNESS_POLL_SECONDS replaces the 20 s poll interval (tests).

gate prints the auto-land verdict (harness_lib/autoland.py) and exits 0 whatever it is; a gh or git call that
fails gives `wait: <call> failed: <first stderr line>`.

Exit status: 0 done; 1 a bad invocation, a failed precondition or call (one line on stderr), failed checks, or
a land whose cleanup stopped; 8 checks still pending (as gh pr checks).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime as dt
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
from pathlib import Path

